<%# One set, warmup or working. #598.

    A warmup row and a working-set row are the same row, which #207 argued and the panel
    around this still says above its lists. They were two copies of it in _lift_panel.erb,
    fifty-five lines byte for byte and an edit form differing in two characters, with the
    reasoning for that form written above one copy only -- so the comment that stops #114 and
    #141 coming back sat over half of the places the fix lives. One row now, one copy of every
    argument, and each thing that differs is written as a difference.

    What differs is small. A working set can carry a target RPE and be rated; a warmup never
    does either, and both guards below already answer that from the set itself, so nothing
    here has to be told which kind it is drawing. What remains is layout: a warmup's plate
    math and dumbbells sit beside its load and a working set's sit under it, and a warmup's
    edit form is a little tighter. Those are kept exactly as they were rather than decided
    here. #207 argues they should converge; that is a visual change and worth making on
    purpose, not as a side effect of moving markup. %>
<%# data-set-row is how the offline banner finds this row again (#516). It knows
    which set a failed tap was about from the path the request went to, which is a
    fact that survives the swap the element that sent it does not, and this is what
    turns that set id back into somewhere on the screen to say so.

    An attribute rather than a node remembered in a closure, because the row it has
    to mark is very often not the row that was there when the tap failed: every
    fifteen-second poll re-renders all five panels, and a mark has to be re-applied
    to whatever element is standing in this set's place afterwards. %>
<li data-set-row="<%= set[:id] %>" class="mt-2 rounded-xl border px-3 py-3 <%= row_style(set) %>">
  <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
    <div>
      <span class="text-xl font-medium text-gray-900">
        <%= load_label(set) %>
      </span>
      <%# Same words and same treatment whichever kind of set this is: a warmup and a
          working set are loaded off the same rack, so the line that says how is not allowed
          to be a different thing in the two places. Beside the load on a warmup and under it
          on a working set, which is the layout difference the note at the top keeps. A span
          either way, `block` where it was a div, so the element does not depend on the kind. %>
      <%- if set[:is_barbell] %>
        <span class="<%= set[:is_warmup] ? 'ml-2 inline-block' : 'block' %> text-xs text-gray-500">
          <span class="uppercase tracking-wide">Plate math</span>
          <%= plate_label(set) %>
        </span>
      <%- end %>
      <%# What the load on a dumbbell row is the weight of. #502. The same slot and the same
          register as the plate math, because it answers the same question -- what do I pick
          up -- for the rack the plate math cannot speak for. Silent unless the movement has
          said how many it is done with; see dumbbell_label. %>
      <%- if (dumbbells = dumbbell_label(set)) %>
        <span class="<%= set[:is_warmup] ? 'ml-2 inline-block' : 'block' %> text-xs text-gray-500">
          <span class="uppercase tracking-wide">Dumbbells</span>
          <%= dumbbells %>
        </span>
      <%- end %>
      <%# What the block asked this set to feel like (#265), under the load and above
          the five buttons that answer it. Between the two on purpose: it is the
          other half of the prescription, so it belongs with the load rather than
          with the answer, and reading down the row goes asked-for, asked-for,
          answer.

          Small caps in gray-500 is this screen's label register -- Warmup, RPE and
          Plate math all wear it -- so "Target" is recognisable as a label and the
          number after it as a value. Without that the row would carry two bare RPE
          numbers a centimetre apart, one prescribed and one recorded, with nothing
          saying which was which.

          Only on a set the generator wrote a target onto, which is a working set of
          a loaded lift counted in reps. A warmup never carries one, and neither the
          editor nor the tools will write a target on timed or unweighted work, so
          this line cannot appear on a row with no rating buttons beneath it. %>
      <%- if set[:planned_rpe] %>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">
          <span class="uppercase tracking-wide">Target</span> RPE <%= set[:planned_rpe] %>
        </div>
      <%- end %>
      <%# How the room is set up for this lift. #412.

          Its own line under the load rather than another clause on it, which is where
          it differs from "per side" and "commanded": those say what the *set* is and
          belong in the phrase a lifter reads at arm's length, and this says what to do
          to the bench and the rack before touching the bar. It is also the one line
          here that is read once and then not again for the rest of the lift.

          The same grey as the plate math above it, because it is the same kind of
          thing: a fact about how to load and stand up this set, read after the number
          rather than instead of it.

          Silent where nothing was prescribed, which is almost every set. A row saying
          "bench —" on every barbell curl would be worse than the note this replaces. %>
      <%- if Tectonic::Setup.phrase(set) %>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500"><%= Tectonic::Setup.phrase(set) %></div>
      <%- end %>
      <%- if changed_from_plan?(set) %>
        <%# Grey, and not the amber it wore until #214. This line is the whole of how
            a changed set is now marked -- the row tint went to two states, done and
            not -- and it says what changed in words, which a colour never did. Grey
            is what the plate-math line above it uses, and this is the same kind of
            thing: a fact about the set, read after the load rather than instead.

            It used to be shown only on a completed set, which was fine while saving
            a correction completed one. #215 stopped that, so the guard had to go
            with it: a set corrected to 145 and not yet done is exactly when you most
            want to see that the sheet said 150. An untouched set cannot reach here
            anyway -- the generator writes weight equal to planned_weight, so
            changed_from_plan? is false until something changes it. %>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">
          planned <%= planned_label(set) %>
        </div>
      <%- end %>
    </div>
    <%# hx-disabled-elt is on this form and not on the others, and the reason it is
        here has changed. #236 put it on because this was the one form whose repeat
        was destructive: a bare tap toggled, so a second tap while the first was
        still in flight landed as an undo and the set you did read as not done.
        Since #542 the tap says which state it wants, so a repeat of it is a repeat
        of a statement and changes nothing -- and this stays, now about the lifter
        rather than the database. A button that goes dead for the length of the
        request is the only thing on this screen saying the first tap was heard, and
        without it a deliberate second tap would feel broken rather than do nothing.
        The revision forms write the weight you typed and the RPE buttons write the
        rating you chose; sending either twice is the same as sending it once. %>
    <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete" method="post"
          hx-post="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete"
          hx-disabled-elt="find button"
          hx-target="#lift-panel-<%= position %>" hx-swap="outerHTML">
      <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/sets/#{set[:id]}/complete") %>
      <%# What this tap is asking for, rather than an instruction to flip. #542.

          The form is rendered from the row, so it already knows which of the two
          words its button is showing -- three lines below, in exactly this
          expression -- and saying that out loud costs nothing and no query. What it
          buys is a request that can be sent twice: a tap held on a phone with no
          signal and replayed when it comes back may land on a set the server already
          recorded, and "flip it" would then un-do the set it had just saved. The set
          edit form has sent an explicit state all along; this is the two of them
          finally agreeing, which is the fix #516 found twelve lines from the bug.

          A value rather than a checkbox's presence, which is what the edit form
          uses, because this field needs three answers and a checkbox has two:
          absence is already spoken for here as the toggle a no-JS post falls back
          to. %>
      <input type="hidden" name="is_completed" value="<%= set[:is_completed] ? 'false' : 'true' %>">
      <%# The name a screen reader hears, which the visible word cannot be. #338: a
          twelve-set session draws twelve buttons called "Done", and a list of the
          page's controls read as "Done, button" twelve times with no way to tell the
          squat from the bench. The label says what this one acts on; the visible
          text stays one word, because that is what makes the screen usable at arm's
          length with chalk on. %>
      <button type="submit"
              aria-label="<%= set[:is_completed] ? 'Undo' : 'Done' %>: <%= accessible_name(set) %>"
              class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 border px-5 py-3 text-base font-semibold
                     <%= complete_style(set) %>">
        <%= set[:is_completed] ? 'Undo' : 'Done' %>
      </button>
    </form>
  </div>

  <%# Where a tap that failed is written down. #516, and the argument is in
      _offline_banner.erb, which is what shows this.

      Words rather than a colour, which is the answer #214 already settled for this
      row: the tint has two states, done and not, and a set finished differently
      from the plan says so in a line of text instead of claiming a third. A set the
      app failed to save is not a third state of the set either -- the set is
      exactly as unfinished as the row already shows -- it is a statement about the
      tap, and it is the one statement on this screen that a lifter cannot make for
      themselves. Amber because this is a warning, which is the single job the
      palette reserves that colour for.

      Under the row rather than beside the button, full width, because it has to be
      readable at arm's length by somebody out of breath and the space beside a Done
      button is a column two words wide.

      Hidden in markup rather than by a class, so the degradation is honest: with no
      JavaScript nothing here ever appears, which is right, because with no
      JavaScript there is no htmx either -- Done is a plain form post and a failed
      one is the browser's own error page rather than a row that goes quiet. %>
  <%# And the line it wears in the ordinary case since #542, which is a different
      claim about the same tap: it is not in the database and it is not lost either,
      it is on the phone waiting for a way out. Two elements rather than one whose
      words the script rewrites, so that both sentences are in the template, beside
      the row they appear on, where they can be read and changed together.

      Grey rather than the amber above it, and that is the palette's rule rather than
      a shade chosen by eye: amber is this app's warning colour and a held tap is not
      a warning. It is the same grey as the plate math and the planned line, which is
      the register this row uses for facts about itself. %>
  <p data-waiting hidden class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-700">
    Waiting to send.
  </p>
  <p data-unsaved hidden class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-amber-800">
    Not saved.
  </p>
  <%# Rated per set, not just per session: five lifts in a session are five
      different questions, and the answer decays within seconds of racking the
      bar. Submitting a rating completes the set, because rating one is saying
      you lifted it.

      Only where a rating means something, which is #211. RPE is reps in reserve,
      so a set counted in seconds has none to be in reserve of and is not asked --
      a plank used to get the same five buttons as a squat, under a scale written
      entirely in rep counts. ratable? is the rule, and it is on the model rather
      than here because the MCP tools and the database constraint enforce the same
      one.

  Never on a warmup, which ratable? also answers. A warmup is submaximal by definition,
  so its rating is a number nobody will read back, and five buttons at 48px would cost
  each of those rows more height than the whole row costs now. %>
  <%- if set.ratable? %>
  <%# A group, so the "RPE" caption is announced with the buttons rather than as loose
      text beside them -- it was a bare span, attached to nothing, so even the scale
      went unsaid. #338. %>
  <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete" method="post"
        hx-post="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete"
        role="group" aria-labelledby="rpe-label-<%= set[:id] %>"
        hx-target="#lift-panel-<%= position %>" hx-swap="outerHTML" class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
    <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/sets/#{set[:id]}/complete") %>
    <span id="rpe-label-<%= set[:id] %>" class="mr-1 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">RPE</span>
    <%# Rated 45 times in a session, one-handed, with chalk on. flex-1 divides the
        row rather than leaving dead space, so the targets are as wide as the
        screen allows; min-h-12 is 48px, which clears the 44px minimum.

        min-w-11 is the other half of that minimum, and it is what makes the row safe
        to widen for #442. flex-1 alone sets a basis of zero and will shrink a button
        to whatever is left, so a set prescribed at RPE 4 would have drawn seven
        buttons about 38px wide -- fixing the missing rating by making every rating
        harder to hit. With a floor of 44px and flex-wrap, the extra buttons take a
        second line instead and every target keeps its size. %>
    <%- rpe_choices(set).each do |rating| %>
      <%# Named per button as well as grouped, because the complaint in #338 is a
          screen reader's *list* of controls -- sixty entries reading "6, button",
          "7, button" -- and a list is exactly where the group's label is lost. %>
      <button value="<%= rating %>" aria-label="RPE <%= rating %> for <%= accessible_name(set) %>"
              class="<%= rpe_style(set, rating) %> min-h-12 min-w-11 flex-1 rounded-lg border border-gray-300 text-base font-semibold" type="submit" name="rpe">
        <%= rating %>
      </button>
    <%- end %>
  </form>
  <%- end %>
  <%# A warmup used to offer Done and nothing else, so a ramp taken differently could
      not be said. That is not a small gap: the ramp is computed from a percentage of
      the top set, and a lifter who adds a step, starts on a 35 lb bar, or finds 115
      heavy and repeats it leaves the session claiming a ramp nobody lifted. row_style
      and changed_from_plan? were already willing to tint such a row amber -- the
      generator writes planned_weight and planned_reps for warmups exactly as it does
      for working sets -- and there was simply no way to make one.

      The disclosure is the working set's, down to the wording, because a warmup and a
      working set are the same act at different weights and learning two habits for it
      is one too many. Closed it stays as quiet as the old row was. The boxes inside
      are the working set's size too: the rule above is that a warmup is played down
      by being smaller, but it applies to what is read, not to what is typed one
      handed with chalk on. %>
  <details class="group <%= set[:is_warmup] ? 'mt-1' : 'mt-2' %>">
    <summary class="flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer items-center text-xs text-gray-500"><%== render('_chevron') %>Lifted something else</summary>
    <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete" method="post"
          hx-post="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/complete"
          hx-target="#lift-panel-<%= position %>" hx-swap="outerHTML" class="<%= set[:is_warmup] ? 'mt-1' : 'mt-2' %> flex items-end gap-2">
      <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/sets/#{set[:id]}/complete") %>
      <%# step="5" here was never a 5 lb grid. HTML counts steps from a step base,
          which is the min attribute if there is one and otherwise the value
          attribute -- and value is this row's own weight. So a row sitting at 138
          accepted 143 and 133 and refused 135, 140 and 145, which is the refusal in
          the screenshot on #114. A rack with 1 lb plates has an increment of 2, so
          weights like 138 are what the generator writes for anybody who owns micro
          plates: the trap fires on ordinary data, not on a corner case.

          min="0" pins the base at zero so a row's own weight can never define the
          grid again, and it rules out a negative load while it is there.

          step is "any" since #141 widened sets.weight to numeric(7, 2). It was 1,
          and the reason was the column: posting "137.5" against an integer got
          PG::InvalidTextRepresentation, the set was left exactly as it was, and htmx
          does not swap a 500 -- so Save appeared to do nothing at all, with no bubble
          to explain it. Refusing half a pound in the browser, where it cost one
          message, beat accepting it and losing the whole set. The column holds it
          now, so the refusal has nothing left to protect.

          "any" rather than "0.25", because this box records what was lifted rather
          than what was prescribed. A machine stack is whatever it is, and a lifter
          who has just moved a pin has no reason to argue with the browser about
          whether the number is on somebody's grid. What a rack can be loaded to is a
          different question, asked at generation, and #140 is where it is answered. %>
      <label class="text-xs text-gray-500" for="weight-<%= set[:id] %>">
        Weight
        <input value="<%= weight_label(set[:weight]) %>" id="weight-<%= set[:id] %>" name="weight" type="number" min="0" step="any" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off"
               class="<%= field_style %> mt-1 block w-24 py-2 text-center text-lg text-gray-900">
      </label>
      <%# Seconds for a set held for time, reps for one counted: a plank offered a Reps box, and a
          lifter typing how long they held it into the only box there was sent a rep count onto
          a set measured in seconds, which the database refuses -- a 500 on the gym floor. The
          set's own measure decides, as it does on the set edit form (#213). %>
      <%- if set.timed? %>
        <label class="text-xs text-gray-500" for="seconds-<%= set[:id] %>">
          Seconds
          <input value="<%= set[:duration_seconds] %>" id="seconds-<%= set[:id] %>" name="duration_seconds" type="number" min="0" step="1" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off"
                 class="<%= field_style %> mt-1 block w-20 py-2 text-center text-lg text-gray-900">
        </label>
      <%- else %>
        <label class="text-xs text-gray-500" for="reps-<%= set[:id] %>">
          Reps
          <input value="<%= set[:reps] %>" id="reps-<%= set[:id] %>" name="reps" type="number" min="0" step="1" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off"
                 class="<%= field_style %> mt-1 block w-20 py-2 text-center text-lg text-gray-900">
        </label>
      <%- end %>
      <%# Saving an edit is the same kind of thing as tapping Done -- an action,
          not a state -- so it wears the same fill. Its hover used to jump to
          lime-500, which turned a blue button green under the thumb and said the
          set was done before it was. %>
      <button type="submit"
              class="<%= button_style %> border border-sky-800 bg-sky-800 px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-sky-900">
        Save
      </button>
    </form>
  </details>
</li>
